    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d180">

                <pb facs="0227" n="1061" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1061"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/10171</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Sharp</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-09-19T12:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 19, 1918—noon.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 1.55 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5120. War Trade Board [from McFadden]:</p>
                <p>No. 427. The following resolution was adopted by the Inter-Allied Blacklisting
                    Committee, Paris: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Inter-Allied Blacklisting, Paris, recognizing the necessity of complete
                            understanding between the Associated Governments as to the date of
                            publication of decisions taken both by the Paris committee and by the
                            committees in London and Washington, has decided that a delay of ten
                            days shall be given each Government in which to state if said Government
                            has any objection to the decisions taken by this committee. If no
                            objection has been registered before the ten days, the decisions will
                            become binding and the names decided upon shall be published in the next
                            bi-weekly lists.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>The above resolution was passed as it had been noted by the Paris committee that
                    names adopted at a meeting were not published simultaneously by the Associated
                    Governments. Please instruct if you approve. McFadden.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
